<h2 id="why">Why test your speakers</h2>
<p>After cleaning, the best way to know whether your speaker is fully back to normal is to listen to it with a predictable signal. Music is complicated and can hide problems, while a clean test tone makes rattles, buzzing and missing channels obvious. This page offers two tests: a <strong>left / right channel test</strong> that plays a tone from each side in turn, and a <strong>frequency sweep</strong> that glides from 50 Hz up to 16 kHz so you can hear how your speaker handles low, middle and high pitches.</p>

<h2 id="lr">How the left / right test works</h2>
<p>The channel test plays a 440 Hz tone for three seconds on the left channel, three seconds on the right, and three seconds on both. It then repeats the pattern at 660 Hz. The display shows which channel is active at every moment, so you can compare what you hear with what should be playing.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stereo phones:</strong> Hold the phone in landscape. One speaker is usually at the bottom edge and the other is combined with the earpiece. Depending on how you rotate the phone, the left channel may come from either end, because many phones swap channels to match orientation.</li>
<li><strong>Single-speaker phones:</strong> You will hear the tone during all three parts because the phone mixes both channels into one speaker. That is normal.</li>
<li><strong>Headphones and laptops:</strong> The test is a quick way to confirm left and right are wired correctly. Keep the volume low with headphones and never use the cleaning modes while wearing them.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="sweep">How the frequency sweep works</h2>
<p>The sweep starts at 50 Hz and rises smoothly to 16,000 Hz over 24 seconds, with the current frequency shown live in the tool. Small phone speakers cannot reproduce the very lowest notes, so the beginning may be faint or silent. The sound should then grow clearer through the middle range and finish with a thin, high whistle. Many adults cannot hear the final few seconds above 14–15 kHz; that is a normal part of how hearing changes with age, not a speaker fault.</p>
<p>The sweep uses the speaker selector, so you can test the left speaker, the right speaker or both separately.</p>

<h2 id="listen">What to listen for</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">What you hear</th><th scope="col">Likely cause</th><th scope="col">What to try</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Buzz or rattle at low pitches only</td><td>A droplet or loose particle in the grille</td><td>Run the water eject or dust cleaner, then retest</td></tr>
<tr><td>Muffled sound across the whole sweep</td><td>Water film or packed lint over the mesh</td><td>Auto clean cycle, gentle brushing, air drying</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crackling at every pitch and volume</td><td>Possible torn diaphragm or loose connection</td><td>Repair service</td></tr>
<tr><td>No sound from one channel on a stereo phone</td><td>Blocked or failed speaker on that side, or a mono audio setting</td><td>Check accessibility audio settings, clean that side, retest</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sound cuts out at certain pitches</td><td>Software processing or a damaged speaker</td><td>Restart the phone, update software, retest</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="steps">Step-by-step: test after cleaning</h2>
<ol>
<li>Let the phone sit for a few minutes after any cleaning run so loosened water can drain.</li>
<li>Set the media volume to about 50 percent. A test does not need to be loud.</li>
<li>Select <strong>Left / right test</strong> and tap Start. Confirm you hear each side when the label says so.</li>
<li>Select <strong>Frequency sweep</strong>, choose Both, and tap Start. Listen for buzzing or sudden drops in volume.</li>
<li>If one side sounds worse, choose that side in the selector and repeat the sweep to compare.</li>
<li>Go back to the cleaning tools for another pass if needed, then test again.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="settings">Settings that can affect the test</h2>
<p>Some phone settings change how channels are played. <strong>Mono audio</strong> in accessibility settings combines left and right, so both speakers play the same thing. Audio enhancers, equalisers and "Dolby" style effects can change the loudness of certain pitches. Bluetooth devices take over the output entirely, so disconnect them first. On iPhone, silent mode must be off for web audio to play through the speaker.</p>
